course: dictionary
lesson: 12146
title: Comparing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Comparing means looking at two or more things to see how they are alike or different, or which one is bigger. «We are comparing the lengths of two pencils» means we are checking which pencil is longer.
explain: Comparing is the -ing form of the verb compare. We use -ing when the action is going on right now or over a period of time. «Sam is comparing the prices of two bikes» means Sam is looking at both prices at this moment.
explain: Look for the clues in the sentence. Words like «alike», «different», «bigger», «smaller», «more» or «less» often tell you that someone is comparing. When you see them, ask yourself: what two things are being put side by side?
fact: Comparing means looking at things to see how they are alike or different.
fact: Comparing also means looking at things to see which one is bigger, or smaller.
fact: Comparing is the -ing form of the verb compare.
fact: The other forms of compare are compares, compared, and compare.
fact: «Ella is comparing 1/2 and 3/4» means she is checking which fraction is bigger.
fact: «The scientists are comparing the results of their fair test» means they are looking at their results side by side to see what is alike and what is different.
exercise: «We are comparing the lengths of two pencils.» What does comparing mean here? | Cutting the pencils into pieces | *Looking at two pencils to see which is longer | Drawing a picture of a pencil | Buying two new pencils
exercise: Which word is the -ing form of compare? | compares | compared | compare | *comparing
exercise: «Sam is comparing the prices of two bikes.» Why is Sam doing this? | To ride both bikes at once | To paint the bikes | *To find out which bike costs less | To count the wheels on each bike
test: «Ella is comparing 1/2 and 3/4.» What is Ella doing? | Adding the two fractions together | *Looking at the two fractions to see which is bigger | Writing a story about fractions | Drawing a picture of a dog
test: «The scientists are comparing the results of their fair test.» What does this sentence tell you? | They are sleeping after the test | They are throwing their results away | *They are looking at their results side by side to see what is alike and different | They are writing a new test
exam: Jo says «comparing» is a noun. Is she right? | *No, it is the -ing form of the verb compare | Yes, it is the name of a thing | Yes, it means a bike or a bag | No, it is a number
